====Conjunction====
{{en-head|conj}}

# {{n-g|Connects at least two alternative [[word]]s, [[phrase]]s, [[clause]]s, [[sentences]], [[etc.]], each of which could make a passage [[true]].}}
#: {{ux|en|He might get cancer, '''or''' be hit by a bus, '''or''' God knows what.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} {{n-g|An operator denoting the [[disjunction]] of two [[propositions]] or [[truth values]]. There are two forms: the [[inclusive or]] and the [[exclusive or]].}}
# {{n-g|Used to present two or more options from which a choice is to be made.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you want tea '''or''' coffee?}}
#: {{ux|en|You may stay '''or''' go.}}
# [[otherwise|Otherwise]] {{gloss|a consequence of the condition that the previous is false}}.
#: {{ux|en|It's raining! Come inside '''or''' you'll catch a cold!}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=IV|page=46|passage=No matter how early I came down, I would find him on the veranda, smoking cigarettes, '''or''' otherwise his man would be there with a message to say that his master would shortly join me if I would kindly wait.}}
# {{n-g|Connects two equivalent names.}}
#: {{ux|en|The country [[Myanmar]], '''or''' [[Burma]]}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} ''Used in the title of a work after a short name to introduce an alternative name, generally one which is more descriptive. Typically preceded by a semicolon or colon and followed by a comma.''
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Isaac Newton|title={{w|Opticks|Opticks: '''or''', A Treatise of the Reflexions, Refractions, Inflexions and Colours of Light}}|year=1704|nocolon=1|page_plain=title page|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/opticksortreatisnewt/page/n8/mode/1up}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Eliza Haywood|title={{w|Love in Excess; or, The Fatal Enquiry|Love in Excess; '''or''' The Fatal Enquiry}}|year=1719|nocolon=1|page_plain=title page|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/loveinexcessorfa00hayw/page/n4/mode/1up}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Samuel Richardson|title={{w|Pamela; or, Virtue Rewarded|Pamela; '''or''', Virtue Rewarded}}|year=1740|nocolon=1|page_plain=title page|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/richardsonpamela3ed04/page/n5/mode/1up}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Erasmus Darwin|title={{w|Zoonomia|Zoonomia; '''or''', the Laws of Organic Life}}|year=1794|nocolon=1|page_plain=title page|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/zoonomiaorlawsof1794darw/page/n4/mode/1up}}
# {{n-g|Used in between two [[numbers]] to denote a [[range]] between them.}}
#: {{ux|en|A man of 18 '''or''' 20 years}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{s|connecting alternative terms}} When not implied by the meaning of the [[conjoin#Noun|conjoin]]s, it is generally ambiguous whether “or” is intended in an exclusive or inclusive sense. In speech, various means may be used to convey exclusivity, such as stress on the word “or” or a rising intonation before it.<ref>{{cite-book|en|last=Huddleston|first=Rodney|year=1988|title=English Grammar: An Outline|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=0521311527|pages=198–99}}</ref> In a formal or technical register, {{m|en|and/or}} may be used to specify inclusivity.

